Based on the provided code, it seems like the comments are asking for assert statements that can verify that the `IndexOf` method in `ArrayList` class will return `-1` if the list does not contain the specified element - which would mean the count of items in the list remains same. Here's a C# debug.assert statement that can verify this:

```cs
Debug.Assert(!(arrList.IndexOf(x) < 0 && arrList.Count != Old_arrListCount));
```
This assert statement will pass if `IndexOf` returns a non-negative value or if the count of items in the list does not change when an item that is not in the list is queried. Otherwise, it will fail. The `!` is used to invert the condition, so the assertion will pass if the item is not found in the list and the count doesn't change.
--------------------Based on the given `ArrayListContractTest` class, it seems that the assertion statement should be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```

This assert statement checks if the return value (`New_Ret`) is negative (which would mean the item is not in the list), or if the count of the list remains the same before and after the `IndexOf()` method is called. 

If the condition is not satisfied, an AssertionFailedException will be thrown.
